-    <!-- The Brief -->
-    <div class="proc-section">
-      <div class="sl">The Brief</div>
-      <div class="proc-brief">
-        <div class="proc-brief-card asked">
-          <div class="proc-brief-num">01</div>
-          <div class="proc-brief-tag" style="color:var(--muted)">What they asked for</div>
-          <div class="proc-brief-title" style="color:rgba(17,17,17,0.85)">A refreshed identity that told the whole story.</div>
-          <p class="proc-brief-body">Applewise had the heritage, the product, and the artisan values. The brief was to bring all of it forward - Scottish roots, handcrafted quality, the apple pies they were famous for. Everything, visible, in one place.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="proc-brief-card meant">
-          <div class="proc-brief-num" style="color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 6%,transparent)">02</div>
-          <div class="proc-brief-tag" style="color:var(--accent)">What it really meant</div>
-          <div class="proc-brief-title">Don't include everything. Find the one thing.</div>
-          <p class="proc-brief-body" style="color:rgba(17,17,17,0.8)">The real challenge wasn't how to show more - it was how to reduce. A stronger identity wouldn't come from accumulation. It would come from finding the single idea that contained everything else.</p>
-        </div>
-      </div>
-    </div>
-
-    <!-- Directions Explored -->
-    <div class="proc-section">
-      <div class="sl">Directions Explored</div>
-      <div class="proc-dirs">
-        <div class="proc-dir rejected">
-          <div class="proc-dir-ghost">✕</div>
-          <div class="proc-dir-status"><span class="proc-dir-indicator"></span>Ruled Out</div>
-          <div class="proc-dir-title">Five directions. Five dead ends.</div>
-          <p class="proc-dir-body">Five routes were explored in full. Heritage-led (tartan shield, Scottish crest) communicated origin but buried the product. Product-led (illustrated pie) was clear but flat - nothing to make it stick. Craft aesthetics (rolling pins, wheat, rustic type) felt borrowed. Combining everything - tartan, pie, thistle, apple - answered the brief literally and failed it creatively. A modern minimal mark was clean but had no story. Each direction worked on its own terms. None unified the brand.</p>
-          <div class="proc-dir-verdict">↳ Ruled out - the right answer wasn't in any single direction. It was between two of them.</div>
-        </div>
-        <div class="proc-dir chosen">
-          <div class="proc-dir-ghost" style="color:var(--accent)">✓</div>
-          <div class="proc-dir-status"><span class="proc-dir-indicator"></span>Chosen Direction</div>
-          <div class="proc-dir-title">Two ideas. One mark.</div>
-          <p class="proc-dir-body">The apple pie lattice and the MacLeod tartan share the same underlying geometry - a repeating grid of interlocking lines. Overlaid, they don't compete: they become one cohesive form. Product and heritage, resolved into a single mark that needs no explanation, but rewards the eye. The answer wasn't to include more. It was to find where two things were already the same thing.</p>
-          <div class="proc-dir-verdict">↳ Chosen - because the best solution doesn't add elements together. It finds where they already overlap.</div>
-        </div>
-      </div>
-    </div>
